ntfs-3g (ntfs-macos) patch 0009: xattr names are exempt from windows_names (macOS only)

Rationale (T-M3-23, ADR-025, docs/spikes/M3-xattr.md finding 8): with windows_names,
ntfs_fuse_setxattr in src/ntfs-3g.c calls ntfs_forbidden_chars() on the stream name that
streams_interface=openxattr maps the xattr name to. The check refuses : / \ * ? " < > |, control
characters and a trailing dot or space, with EINVAL. macOS uses ':' in the names of Finder tags
(com.apple.metadata:_kMDItemUserTags), Finder comments (com.apple.metadata:kMDItemFinderComment) and
com.apple.metadata:com_apple_backup_excludeItem. With the check, setting a tag fails and copying a file that
carries such an xattr onto the volume fails (ditto creates no file, cp -p leaves the file without the
xattr, copyfile(COPYFILE_ALL) leaves an empty file).
Product choice (owner decision 2026-11-15): windows_names stays on for file and directory names and is
not applied to xattr names. The check protects names that Windows cannot open by path. An xattr name is
never typed by a Windows user and never becomes a path on macOS, so the check gives no protection here.
Hunks (src/ntfs-3g.c, two: ntfs_fuse_setxattr and ntfs_fuse_removexattr):
  - Under "#if defined(__APPLE__) || defined(__DARWIN__)" the ntfs_forbidden_chars() test is not made.
  - One guard stays: an empty stream name (lename_len == 0, for example the name "user." or an empty
    open-namespace name) is refused with EINVAL. ntfs_forbidden_chars() refused it by its "len == 0" rule,
    and without the guard the call would address the unnamed data stream, that is the file content.
  - The error path for lename_len == -1 (name conversion failure) is unchanged.
  - ntfs_fuse_removexattr gets the same empty-name guard (M3 audit F-07): upstream passes lename_len == 0 to
    ntfs_attr_remove(ni, AT_DATA, lename, lename_len), which would remove the unnamed data stream, that is
    the file content. The call cannot reach it today (the macOS VFS refuses an empty xattr name with EINVAL
    before any file system sees it), so this is defence in depth, in the same style as the setxattr guard.
  - The #else branches are the upstream text, so the Linux build (Environment A, internal FUSE) compiles the
    same code as before.
Not changed: file and directory names (the ntfs_forbidden_names() calls in ntfs_fuse_create and
ntfs_fuse_link, and in libntfs-3g) keep the windows_names check (I-NAME-04). ntfs_fuse_removexattr and
ntfs_fuse_getxattr do not check the name for forbidden characters (T-M3-08: an existing ':' stream is read and
removed under windows_names), so they need no change for that; removexattr gets only the empty-name guard above. The stream-name length stays limited by the library
(NTFS_MAX_NAME_LEN, 255 UTF-16 units; macOS refuses names above 127 bytes before they reach the worker).
Scope: FUSE glue only, no change to libntfs-3g, allocation, runlists, MFT or index code. src/lowntfs-3g.c
is not patched and is not shipped (patch 0003).
Open question (U23, Environment C check H-USB-11): whether Windows and chkdsk keep a stream whose name
contains ':' on a volume written this way. Windows cannot address such a stream by path. Fallback if
chkdsk drops or flags it: revert this patch and document that Finder tags, comments and backup exclusion
are not stored and that copying a file that carries them fails.
Note: on macOS the exemption covers the stream name under both streams_interface=xattr and
openxattr (the product uses openxattr). getxattr has no empty-name guard (it only
reads; that is upstream code and an empty name cannot reach it from the kernel).
Upstream status: product choice, not to be reported upstream (ADR-025).
Test: I-XATTR-05 (':' and other Windows-invalid xattr names stored and read back under windows_names;
file and directory names with the same characters still refused), I-XATTR-06 (ditto, cp -p, cp -c and
copyfile(COPYFILE_ALL) of a file with Finder tags and comment keep every xattr), I-XATTR-09 (Linux lists
and reads the ':' stream). Cases in tests/integration/xattr/, Environment B; I-XATTR-09 runs with
run-env-b.sh --orchestrate. I-NAME-04 still refuses the same file and directory names.

--- a/src/ntfs-3g.c	2026-11-16 09:42:19.434998311 +0000
+++ b/src/ntfs-3g.c	2026-11-16 09:42:28.347813684 +0000
@@ -3602,12 +3602,22 @@
 	}
 #endif
 	lename_len = fix_xattr_prefix(name, namespace, &lename);
+#if defined(__APPLE__) || defined(__DARWIN__)
+	/* ntfs-macos 0009: windows_names applies to file names, not xattr names */
+	if (lename_len == 0)
+		errno = EINVAL; /* an empty name would address the unnamed stream */
+	if ((lename_len == -1) || (lename_len == 0)) {
+		res = -errno;
+		goto exit;
+	}
+#else
 	if ((lename_len == -1)
 	    || (ctx->windows_names
 		&& ntfs_forbidden_chars(lename,lename_len,TRUE))) {
 		res = -errno;
 		goto exit;
 	}
+#endif
 	na = ntfs_attr_open(ni, AT_DATA, lename, lename_len);
 	if (na && flags == XATTR_CREATE) {
 		res = -EEXIST;
@@ -3844,7 +3854,14 @@
 	}
 #endif
 	lename_len = fix_xattr_prefix(name, namespace, &lename);
+#if defined(__APPLE__) || defined(__DARWIN__)
+	/* ntfs-macos 0009: an empty name would address the unnamed stream */
+	if (lename_len == 0)
+		errno = EINVAL;
+	if ((lename_len == -1) || (lename_len == 0)) {
+#else
 	if (lename_len == -1) {
+#endif
 		res = -errno;
 		goto exit;
 	}
